{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: exact head 1549f891184039960ec0dfd899ebef1cbc2e0389 on builder/sh1-self-host-screens-20261117, the SH-1 design note, for request 7bfbe9bd under my promise 842ff995.\n\nThe head has one path, notes/2026-11-17-sh1-self-host-screens.md, so it needs no gate under GS-1. Its parent is c17d556d, an ancestor of origin main 69b85e98, and RB-1 changed no path in this head. check-notes gives 0 warnings, and the commit-message check passes with 1 checked and 0 flagged. `git diff --check` is clean.\n\nThe design answers the four questions of the request, and its shape is sound: one process, the sandbox module unchanged, and a factory that keeps the RPC surface. Two places leave a path where the room's own page or the room's trust can reach the screen origin. The note must close both before any source work starts.\n\n1. The celld host dispatch fails open. Section 2 step 1 says \"Every other host goes to the unchanged room `fetch`\". The room does not check the request host against `PUBLIC_ORIGIN`: no room route reads the host. The owner steps then put the screen name into `hosts` and Caddy at steps 4 and 5, but deploy the screen setting only at step 10. Between those steps, `https://screens.room.example/` serves the full room page. A member who opens the room page there enrols a device key in the storage of the screen origin. After step 10, package script runs on that same origin and can read that key. The \"No room keys\" property in section 3 then fails. The same fault returns whenever the setting and the `hosts` list disagree, for example after a redeploy with an old project directory.\n   Required: the dispatch sends only the host of `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the room `fetch`, sends a screen host to the sandbox handler, and answers every other host with 404 (or 421). State that the room page never answers on a screen host, in any configuration state. Add these cases to the planned workerd test: a host that is neither the room nor a screen gets 404, and the screen name before its setting deploys gets 404 on `/` and on the room page path.\n\n2. The browser descriptor rule must not weaken the hosted rule. Today `loadApplicationScreenContext` (`packages/client/src/application-screen.ts:25-30`) requires a 200 descriptor on every `inguz.space` origin. Section 1 says a 404 \"keeps the present same-origin path\" on every `https:` origin. Read literally, a hosted screen whose descriptor answers 404 then reads its context as if the screen origin were the room. Required: state that an `inguz.space` origin still requires a 200 descriptor and fails closed on 404. Add a refused case for it to the planned `application-gateway.test.ts` cases.\n\n3. Name the site relation. The hosted screens sit on `inguz.space`, a different site from the room. A screen name such as `screens.room.example` shares the room's registrable domain, so the two origins are same-site. Screen script can set a cookie for `.room.example` that the room origin then receives. At c17d556d no room, scope or client source reads a `cookie` header or `document.cookie`, so the risk is low today. Required: one sentence in section 3 that states the same-site relation and why it holds (the room reads no cookie and judges every act by its signature), and a pin or test that fails if a room route starts to read cookies. If the pin costs more than the sentence is worth, say so in the note and leave it to the source head.\n\nWhat I checked and found sound:\n\n- The parser rule. `isSeparateScreenOrigin` requires an exact `https:` origin with a DNS hostname, no IP literal and no port, and a hostname that differs from the room's. The existing `roomOrigin !== screenOrigin` check stays. The `deploy-smoke` check keeps every hosted descriptor inside `isApplicationSandboxOrigin`, so the hosted configuration cannot widen.\n- Server-side Host spoofing gains nothing. A request with a forged `Host` reaches either the sandbox's narrowed routes or the room routes it could reach directly. The browser draws the origin boundary from the URL, not from the server's dispatch.\n- The sandbox module runs unchanged and keeps its route checks, its `set-cookie` removal and its removal of `authorization`. The descriptor pin at `apps/sandbox/worker.ts:52-53` compares one setting with itself on celld. The note says so.\n- Wake rate. The design adds no alarm, retry or scheduled pass.\n- Size. Eleven owner steps, one of them with `sudo` and one that re-trusts a certificate on each device. Whether that is onerous is Hugh's call, and the note gives him the list.\n\nArchitecture: sound in shape. Change 1 belongs in the design, because it sets the dispatch rule that the source head implements.\n\nSecurity: changes 1 and 2 are blocking. Change 3 is a required statement.\n\nSimplification: no required change. The factory extraction is the smallest seam that keeps the hosted RPC surface.\n\nThe open questions (`Host` on celld 0.6.3, `.local` resolution) stay as written. This verdict authorizes no source work, push, landing or deployment.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:83424abe12423a76b9c9e0e60abfb1f33330c4e0","binding":"assigned","head":"1549f891184039960ec0dfd899ebef1cbc2e0389","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:842ff995b1076845e31638643c81e9ed02202cdf\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:943a5234a1a6877050c8a9acb60ed7dd43c582ce\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:842ff995b1076845e31638643c81e9ed02202cdf","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}